              "stem": "(ii) Hence sketch \\(y=\\frac1{|x^2-2x-4|}\\), labelling its asymptotes and turning point.",
              "answer": "The vertical asymptotes are \\(x=1-\\sqrt5\\) and \\(x=1+\\sqrt5\\), and the horizontal asymptote is \\(y=0\\). The graph is positive on all four intervals. Between the vertical asymptotes it has a minimum at \\((1,\\frac15)\\); outside them the branches approach zero as \\(|x|\\to\\infty\\).",
              "hint": "Use the zeros of the quadratic for the vertical asymptotes. The absolute value reflects any negative part of the denominator upward, and the reciprocal turns its largest absolute denominator value into the smallest y-value.",
              "keyPoints": [
                {
                  "text": "\\(y=\\frac1{|x^2-2x-4|}\\)",
                  "note": "The absolute value makes the reciprocal positive on both sides of each root."
                },
                {
                  "text": "labelling its asymptotes and turning point",
                  "note": "The denominator roots give vertical asymptotes; the quadratic vertex gives the central minimum."
                }
              ],
              "steps": [
                {
                  "explain": "Use the quadratic roots from part (i) as the vertical asymptotes; the reciprocal tends to zero for large |x|.",
